Good. In that case I'll consider this issue resolved; yes, it's a regression in 0.58.0 since it used to work in MinTTY and cmd.exe, and no longer does now, but I don't think it's a serious problem when Windows Terminal is better than those anyway.

If anybody isn't happy with this, feel free to dig deeper. You should then probably file an issue in the [tcell](https://github.com/gdamore/tcell) repo to see if gdamore can do anything about it. I wouldn't hold my breath though, considering what he wrote [here](https://github.com/gdamore/tcell/blob/main/README-windows.md#terminal-emulators). I'm not going to spend any more time on this myself, in any case.

@AlbertoErcolani @vaportail Does that work for you too?

@ManuLinares No; hiding closed PRs for main branches was discussed above, and I'll probably add this soon (on the assumption that a PR on a main branch is almost always a mistake).

For other branches, it is important to see the PR even if closed or merged, because it tells you that you can now delete the branch.

There is one special case that trips me up every time I run into it: creating a new branch and reusing a name that was used before. Happened to me today, the branch was called `fix-failing-test`, and I used that before, a few years ago. In this case, you push the new branch for the first time and it immediately shows a magenta ("merged") icon next to it, which is quite confusing. But there's no way for lazygit to tell that this is a different branch from the one back then, so I don't see what we can do about it.

> I agree that it would be better to model these as arrays rather than quoted strings, and maybe I'll change that some day.

@ahmetsait I take that back actually. It is important that the command string is interpreted by a shell (or cmd.exe on Windows) so that things like pipes, output redirection, command substitution, globbing, and env var expansion work. Granted, none of these are likely to be needed for calling an editor, but they are very much needed in custom commands, and I think we should be consistent with how we call external commands across the app.

@AntoineGS I tried again, and I could now reproduce that editAtLineAndWait failed but the other two worked. Very weird. I think it might have something to do with the total number of quotes in the string or something (e.g. `"C:\My editor.exe" {{filename}}` and `"C:\My editor.exe" "{{filename}}"` might be different). I stopped investigating this more because I don't have much time right now.

I think adding `/s` is the right move to solve this. I tried your branch and it worked for me, so that's nice; didn't look at the code much yet. Just for fun I asked Claude to fix this too, and this resulted in the `fix-windows-cmd-quoting` branch (I didn't look at its code, either). I noticed that they seem to behave differently: for mine, the `/s` and the additional quotes show up in the Log panel, for yours they don't. I think I would prefer it if they did.

In my branch I added another commit that simplifies things and (I think) fixes use of `&&` which was broken before; I think this should fix #2427 (didn't get a chance to try this yet).

Not sure yet how to proceed from here; I don't have much time today to continue, but in general I think I'd prefer to work on this myself rather than review yours. But any input is welcome.

@brianphillips No worries, and thanks for the effort with preparing the PR. I'm still in the process of figuring out how to deal with PRs that are obviously vibe-coded but still useful.

In this case I decided to open a PR of my own instead of reviewing yours: #5596. Mine was also done with heavy AI support, but it's just easier for me to drive the agent myself rather than review other people's AI-generated code.

Please test if this works for you! I don't have access to a GH Enterprise server myself, so couldn't test it.

@aofei Thanks for the PR; since I don't usually accept PRs from new contributors any more, I'm still undecided whether I'll make an exception in this case, or make my own PR to fix it instead. I haven't had a super close look at yours yet, still thinking about it.

@glasser's point is valuable too though, and I feel it's the more important issue actually. If you ask an AI agent to perform a rebase, it can easily happen that you bring lazygit to the foreground at a time where the agent is done resolving conflicts for a given commit, but is still in the process of fixing build errors. Eventually, @aofei's fix will dismiss the popup when the rebase is done, but you also don't want to see it when you're still in the middle of the rebase. I'm thinking that we should simply never open the popup when a rebase was started outside of lazygit (on the assumption that it was probably an agent starting it). For me personally, the only case where I start a rebase outside of lazygit is for something like `git rebase -x "make test" master`, in which case the popup is pointless because lazygit doesn't let you continue a rebase that has pending `exec` todos anyway.

Thoughts welcome.

@prosoitos This is actually not the same at all, it's a very different crash, just has a similar panic message.

Fixed by #5655, thanks for reporting.

You must have `<disabled>` keybindings in your config file, why is that? I'm really just curious how people work.

@rgoya Thanks for testing.

I force-pushed a new version; the bug should be fixed, errors are now shown in a popup rather than just the Commands Log, and I even decided to implement an approval prompt. I think that's good to have, otherwise there could be confusion when users get the error popup, switch to a different terminal and type "direnv allow", and come back to lazygit to find that it still doesn't work; they would have to quit and restart for it to take effect. Doing the in-app approval makes this a lot more seamless.

Please test again!

@scodougy I am not a tmux user and I'm very unfamiliar with it. I just installed it, ran it with no arguments and no config file, and then started lazygit (current master version) inside it, and I don't see a problem: two-finger scrolling on my trackpad scrolls the pane under the mouse. Works fine for the main view. My tmux version is 3.6b.

I wonder if this could be some weird interaction between the terminal and tmux? I'm using ghostty, maybe you want to try if this works better?

@rasadov Thanks, but this project no longer accepts PRs. Help is still very much appreciated, so if you can investigate a bug and find its root cause, this would be great. Just no PRs.

@stefanhaller thanks for your reply about f/h records. I need to acknowledge that my day job doesn't leave much time for other software and so I am going to be very slow to participate here. On a positive note, it does appear that in my LLM-generated solutions, the f/h support was added with relatively few lines of code:

- delta f/h emission: https://github.com/stefanhaller/delta/compare/osc-1717-metadata...dandavison:osc-1717-metadata-extensions?expand=1
- gitu f/h handling: https://github.com/altsem/gitu/commit/0758ae069d1ba1090a1463934ee6521ef75a19fb?w=1

@Schuasda Is the freezing of 1-2 minutes also new with 0.63, or did you see this before too?

If the main thread blocks for such a long time, then I can see how the userEvents channel overflows easily. We refresh the Files panel every 10s, causing several user events to be posted to the userEvents channel, and if they can't be serviced by the main thread then you get the panic. I can reproduce this by adding a time.Sleep of a few minutes into a command handler.

This doesn't explain @rokkerruslan's scenario; we pause background refreshes when suspending to the terminal.

@rokkerruslan @Schuasda Here's a fix, can you please test this thoroughly: #5793. I'd like to release a patch update soon (Sunday or early next week).

> Do you have any idea of what causes the main thread to block for so long?

No, this is not normal. You could run with `-debug` and send the log, maybe it shows it.

@stefanhaller I did but it contains private company info (repo address, local storage, recent commits, etc.), so I don't feel comfortable sharing it.

If you're willing to indicate commands to run or actions to do, I'm open to analyze in on my PC and give you feedback.

First noticeable thing is that the YAML format is probably broken, the file is interpreted as binary format when I open it. I'll do a binary comparison with my current (valid) `state.yml` and check for possible issues or mismatches.

@stefanhaller The issue seems worse than just a bogus character: the file only contains 4196 bytes of NUL characters (verified in both VS Codium and Notepad++), meaning the whole file buffer was erased at some point.

I don't know the codebase or Go language, is there a condition in the file writer functions that could explain that ?
FYI the app is in version `0.63.1` for Windows.

@stefanhaller do you have any thoughts on this? I'm still using [my fork](https://github.com/jknlsn/lazygit/tree/coloured-commit-length-indicator), rebasing occasionally to get the latest updates, and still finding it super useful.

Since the original screenshots I've fixed the counter losing its bold attribute when it goes yellow/red. And now that gocui lives in the repo rather than being an external dependency, the change to make subtitle rendering parse ANSI escape codes sits directly in `pkg/gocui`. I've updated the screenshots in the issue to match.

Happy to open a PR, or address any design feedback first if there's something you'd change!

@haxtibal It's always a good idea to read a project's CONTRIBUTING.md before submitting a PR for it. I don't take PRs here.

The request makes sense though; but it's only half of what's needed. Like for adding a Co-authored-by, there needs to be a way to do it during composition of the commit message; that's #5872 I guess. And this becomes even more important when we remove the config to sign off every commit (see https://github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit/issues/4911#issuecomment-3656780525 for more on that).

I guess the best we can do for this is to add an entry to the commit message editor's `ctrl+o` menu; with a keybinding of `s`, adding a Signed-off-by trailer then takes `ctrl-o`, `s`, which is maybe convenient enough. Opinions welcome, I very rarely use Signed-off-by trailers myself. (For comparison, `git gui` has a "Sign Off" button in its commit message editor, with a Command-S keybinding, so it's a single keystroke. But I don't want to add single-key bindings to the commit message editor, and `ctrl+s` is already taken anyway.

I've updated the version to [0.65.1](https://github.com/nils-a/au-packages/releases/tag/lazygit-0.65.1) and it's currently being reviewed: https://community.chocolatey.org/packages/lazygit/0.65.1

If more problems arise, do not hesitate to open an issue at https://github.com/nils-a/au-packages/.

@stefanhaller I think we can close this issue here. (And probably it's not a bug - not in this repo at least.)

Happens also with git credential-manager + gpg, very annoying since I'd like to use it for rewording other commits beside HEAD.

Also breaks the terminal for me alongside it, so I also need to reset/restart the terminal.

